# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Participant data — NEVER COMMIT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# data/final/ still holds the wide analysis df, which carries direct identifiers
# (phone numbers, name-bearing email addresses, RedCap survey links with access
# tokens, free-text clinical narrative) and 38 timestamp columns.  It must be
# de-identified before any of it is published.  Excluding the whole directory
# rather than individual files so a new export cannot slip through.
# Git will not descend into an excluded directory, so the one cleared file has
# to be reached by excluding contents rather than the directory itself:
# exclude everything in data/, re-admit data/final/ so git descends into it,
# exclude everything inside it, then re-admit the single cleared file.
# Do NOT collapse this to `data/` plus a negation -- `!data/` cancels the
# exclusion wholesale and makes the wide analysis df trackable.
data/*
!data/final/
data/final/*
submission/*

# EXCEPTION - the de-identified recruitment export.  Produced by
# 06_submission/deidentify_recruit_csv.py in the master repository, which keeps
# only the 66 columns the published pipeline reads and replaces every
# identifying column with the derived value the code computes from it
# (phone_number -> phone_cc_234, continue_date -> continue_date_passed,
#  timestamp_survey_bl -> timesincesurveystart_gt14,
#  task_data_prltask -> task_data_prltask_present, qc_notes withheld).
# Verified to reproduce Supplementary Tables S1/S2 and every CONSORT count
# byte-identically.  This is the ONLY file under data/ cleared for release.
!data/final/df_recruit_public_*.csv

# Backups of the wide df — same content, same exclusion.
**/backups/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OS / editor / language caches
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.Rhistory

# Editor / assistant local state
.claude/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generated HPC job bundles — participant-level CSVs, NEVER COMMIT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# generate_hpc_jobs.py writes df_foranalysis_master.csv (+ four byte-identical
# legacy aliases, ~14 MB each) and the SLURM .txt job arrays here.  These are
# rebuilt from scratch on every run and contain the full participant-level
# analysis dataframe, so they must never reach a public repository.
03_hpc/output/

# Model output pulled back from the cluster.
results/

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Regression-harness fixtures — real fitted models, NEVER COMMIT
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 03_hpc/regression_harness/fixtures/ holds brmsfit objects pulled from Bouchet
# so the equivalence tests can call posterior_linpred()/posterior_epred(), which
# need a genuine brmsfit rather than the synthetic draws stub used before.
# Every brmsfit carries its analysis rows in fit$data — participant-level data —
# so these must never reach a public repository.  ~110 MB total.
# Re-fetch them with:  Rscript regression_harness/fetch_fixtures.R
# (the reference CSVs beside them are model summaries, not participant data,
#  but they are pulled by the same command and kept together for provenance)
03_hpc/regression_harness/fixtures/

# 03_hpc/regression_harness/mc_trace_export/ is the Monte-Carlo trace dump written
# by  local_equivalence_test.R --export-trace.  At production L it holds the two
# S x N x L mediator matrices -- 28.9 GB as float64 at S=16000, N=113, L=1000 --
# alongside per-participant linear predictors, so it is both far too large for git
# and derived from participant-level data.  Regenerate with:
#   Rscript local_equivalence_test.R --export-trace --fixtures=<key> --trace-L=1000
# then analyse with mc_trace_analysis.py.  Both scripts ARE tracked; only the
# multi-gigabyte output is not.
03_hpc/regression_harness/mc_trace_export/

# Generated figure output (regenerated by 04_visualizations/0X_all_figures.py).
# figures/ holds the tracked SOURCE assets hgf_alone.png / hgf_alone.svg, which
# the figure-3 assembly reads; everything else under it is assembly output.
# The .svg matters: without it the assembly falls back to
# svgutils.ImageElement(path_str, ...), which calls .read() on its first argument
# and dies with "'str' object has no attribute 'read'", losing the vector figure.
figures/*
!figures/hgf_alone.png
!figures/hgf_alone.svg
figures/caps_vision_hppd_combined_4_figs/
04_visualizations/figures/

# Assembled manuscript figures, regenerated by 0X_all_figures.py. Tracked in the
# master repo because a Google Apps Script syncs them into the manuscript doc;
# that sync does not run against this repo, so they are output here.
04_visualizations/linked_figures/

# Stray R graphics-device artifact, written when a helper opens a device without
# closing it. Not a pipeline output.
Rplots.pdf

# Generated by 03_hpc/generate_nonsp_diagnostic_jobs.py on every figure run
03_hpc/nonsp_diagnostic_jobs.txt

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Submission-only tooling — kept locally, NEVER published
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 06_submission/ holds scripts that exist to prepare and verify the submission
# itself, not to produce a manuscript figure, table or statistic: equivalence
# harnesses, FDR verification, MCMC convergence audits, superseded figure
# variants, and reviewer-response analyses whose results live in the response
# letter rather than the paper.  A reader reproducing the manuscript needs none
# of them, and several read or write participant-level intermediates.  See
# 06_submission/README.md for what each one is and why it was set aside.
06_submission/
